#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84f2706c-12ef-5b31-af15-426be702b5c5" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Variable Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "009e2843-42b0-5ba1-ae2b-ffd2b7ed39c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-1608" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{3y}{y(y+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors.\n\n![](images/51/51bf9631f8b2ca90bda343a84a675f302ebae205446e528f5f05a8ea1f5f577b.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](images/f3/f3edb95bc0a0a57a6dc439a06e1653d95c23dcd49e701b4d4d3f3d8c0f36c594.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{3}{y+1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054496 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38eb9547-3f69-5f6b-a8e3-7034b97a433a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Constant Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "200bcd53-5cfe-5fc4-8efe-6de6d5c180c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-1609" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{20}{8(z-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $20$ in the numerator  becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 5,$ and the $8(z-1)$ in the denominator becomes $2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (z-1).$\n\n![](images/47/47eb2277b9892fbd83cb1ae58c1c2a7640c29326402970254fb119c8c6c9dd5f.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n![](images/28/28f5bd692129118ef790ef1dde224e18f0a70674bf6ccd19eed4dbdb78b7b1f6.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{5}{2(z-1)}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054590 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "750b8a6b-49f4-5ba2-a985-e0153ef8909c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Monomial Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "3b2b92fa-7945-5609-8cc4-7ad230d396df" :question/math-academy-id "e-838" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{6y(x - 2)}{9yz}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6y(x-2)$ in the numerator  becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot (x-2),$ and the $9yz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3 \\cdot y \\cdot z.$\n\n![](images/70/70068644b054adae3915b30b4c8fe3745190ec2a9eef2b1672528189e8dab9fd.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n![](images/38/382b839130e9e8b59572303be4644e8c45c2be55c4b9f45ff2bd664037eecf8e.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2(x - 2)}{3z}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054591 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19e1ac67-8538-5f72-bb0d-b7e4adda36a7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Binomial Factors"}] :question/id #uuid "d076b225-6e63-592e-b03e-2986c1f7685c" :question/math-academy-id "e-839" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac{6a(c+1)}{3b(c+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their prime factors. The $6a(c+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3 \\cdot a \\cdot (c+1),$ and the $3b(c+1)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot b \\cdot (c+1).$\n\n![](images/7d/7d19b8a64cafa7f78bdf3795eba734282ad0a158b26ce12978f474aa12c84211.svg)\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(c+1)$'s.\n\n![](images/72/72b3b315f67d06181cc1457b16fcee0e886c224960030a5975723761513e2371.svg)\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\dfrac{2a}{b}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84f2706c-12ef-5b31-af15-426be702b5c5" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Variable Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d27b27a-980b-5ba5-86b9-9aff77b35144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479405} {:answer/id #uuid "5991eaf9-379d-5279-909a-66c42b65d53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479486} {:answer/id #uuid "77606623-9e12-5922-8bc5-274712f9ce66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479539} {:answer/id #uuid "9775a4ef-c5bc-58e2-beec-931ce7d2fd7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{3z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479589} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3b7392-9245-5b25-b8dd-39ac5d0958a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{z}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5991eaf9-379d-5279-909a-66c42b65d53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479486} :answer-field/id #uuid "36ab8a45-ab87-53fd-b22d-f561bdade95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4044453a-5a6d-5fd7-b8c8-665b004cb36e" :question/math-academy-id "q-100066" :question/problem "What is $\\frac{yz}{3xz}$ reduced to lowest terms?" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{yz}{3xz}=\\frac{y\\cdot z}{3\\cdot x\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $z$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\cdot z}{3\\cdot x\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{y\\cdot \\cancel{z}}{3\\cdot x\\cdot \\cancel{z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{y}{3x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19e1ac67-8538-5f72-bb0d-b7e4adda36a7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Binomial Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c59e113-f08a-53ee-867d-94d7fb5788b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479378} {:answer/id #uuid "11936538-40d4-54b8-8b7b-d33e072de285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479385} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0ec6de-9581-5bd8-bb44-d0aa83898ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479570} {:answer/id #uuid "f4806540-adde-54c6-a865-acf5b657cc39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479724} {:answer/id #uuid "fc830cf0-6f39-543f-9250-0e261d7a0ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc830cf0-6f39-543f-9250-0e261d7a0ffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479739} :answer-field/id #uuid "14fd58b4-2f30-5ee5-84aa-9be98305fb78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "155136a2-7453-5b52-882f-507c2e2bad0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14373" :question/problem "$\\frac{3+2x}{2x+3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that $2x+3=3+2x.$ So we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+2x}{2x+3}=\\frac{3+2x}{3+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3+2x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+2x}{3+2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(3+2x)}}{\\cancel{(3+2x)}} & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38eb9547-3f69-5f6b-a8e3-7034b97a433a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065d2cc6-2b7a-5048-8381-f0d529cf916c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479368} {:answer/id #uuid "630c5016-91ed-523f-b3a0-229839ac925d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479504} {:answer/id #uuid "89dff5ec-8950-5602-9e19-37aace0522e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(x-1)}{(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479564} {:answer/id #uuid "b342417f-d18f-57d0-bb5d-9490f37f2f1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479625} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c5a728-8678-55ce-b083-4259d62dcb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "065d2cc6-2b7a-5048-8381-f0d529cf916c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479368} :answer-field/id #uuid "9063d8ee-9fe6-5e2b-88bd-23fe4635e865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a06dc73c-225f-5c37-b7d5-563ea645e257" :question/math-academy-id "q-155" :question/problem "$\\frac{2(x-1)}{2(x+1)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $2(x-1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot (x-1),$ and the $2(x+1)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot (x+1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-1)}{2(x+1)}=\\frac{2\\cdot (x-1)}{2\\cdot (x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is only one pair of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot (x-1)}{2\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (x-1)}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)}{(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{x-1}{x+1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38eb9547-3f69-5f6b-a8e3-7034b97a433a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb2dabd-d3ff-58f7-863a-fb9e1d9b390d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5(y-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479376} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0cda42-eedf-5058-93d1-bf12ded0aa50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479428} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7d6847-74d4-52eb-a60f-8f439d660902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5(y-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479566} {:answer/id #uuid "90dafb0a-1f40-562d-bd76-33cd101d6a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479579} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b60fe1-7c11-525d-b2b1-13712ed4a16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5(y-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b60fe1-7c11-525d-b2b1-13712ed4a16d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5(y-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479635} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe3e14eb-22b8-5147-9028-3893a72c3d27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b68074c4-93d7-57b0-8864-ec0076cbeea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-41549" :question/problem "$\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $16$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2,$ and the $20(y-3)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2}{5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5(y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{4}{5(y-3)}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "750b8a6b-49f4-5ba2-a985-e0153ef8909c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Monomial Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "362a7338-66ee-57c0-a245-11f4e62738f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479440} {:answer/id #uuid "37115e22-5ffc-53cf-adb7-fa70a8cb055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{4y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479441} {:answer/id #uuid "77eff539-c70f-5cc6-a384-798f4fb1bfae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479540} {:answer/id #uuid "beccfae6-b5ee-5736-b110-8316f9b6d40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479647} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c1f978-3d95-58dc-be98-003e32663ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77eff539-c70f-5cc6-a384-798f4fb1bfae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479540} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac2e58a-247b-5a34-a1a7-53a045e18181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fad200a4-e2f6-57c9-aa77-2766fc482dc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-41552" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{(3x)(7y)}{28y}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $(3x)(7y)$ in the numerator becomes $3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y,$ and the $28y$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3x)(7y)}{28y}=\\frac{3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $7\\text{'s}$ and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{7}\\cdot x\\cdot \\cancel{y}}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{7}\\cdot \\cancel{y}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{3}{4}x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "750b8a6b-49f4-5ba2-a985-e0153ef8909c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Monomial Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2091d8a1-eaab-5a05-8861-1f8abb34b955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479414} {:answer/id #uuid "85fb3187-facc-57f1-ab70-b630841edb64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(5z+1)}{3z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479558} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc04d96-6d76-50b3-a8c9-411832fae8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(5z+1)}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479707} {:answer/id #uuid "f72bdc69-b108-572c-bd30-099ea2502466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(5z+1)}{3z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479730} {:answer/id #uuid "f90a5767-60e8-51c1-8857-0d450ea81290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f72bdc69-b108-572c-bd30-099ea2502466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8(5z+1)}{3z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479730} :answer-field/id #uuid "62023835-2ec7-51b8-9552-f67efdf117fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8919a5c-5e3b-5274-9d2f-901afddd5e90" :question/math-academy-id "q-41557" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{32x(5z+1)}{12xz}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $32x(5z+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1),$ and the $12xz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32x(5z+1)}{12xz}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2\\text{'s}$ and one pair of $x$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(5z+1)}{3z}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{8(5z+1)}{3z}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38eb9547-3f69-5f6b-a8e3-7034b97a433a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277311aa-fc6b-5313-ab2d-b522f597d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053730} {:answer/id #uuid "0368e135-7244-5bd0-b1eb-10f2cbb3ff05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479366} {:answer/id #uuid "0f94d5f2-416f-5904-ba93-5d8d90a6e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479381} {:answer/id #uuid "85810fab-227b-5189-8c9d-62f691a1d160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(x+1)}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479557} {:answer/id #uuid "f38c5c9a-e585-55c7-a855-0be88c9f180c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{4(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f94d5f2-416f-5904-ba93-5d8d90a6e059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479381} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e485d8a-d83d-55b0-a881-7cee8f9d2502" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c99039b2-017b-5b94-ab7c-1d3602f59629" :question/math-academy-id "q-57766" :question/problem "$\\frac{10x}{12(x+1)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $10x$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 5\\cdot x,$ and the $12(x+1)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10x}{12(x+1)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot x}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{5x}{6(x+1)}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84f2706c-12ef-5b31-af15-426be702b5c5" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Variable Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28980dc0-67fe-568a-95f7-85f6561039b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+5z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479419} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4226aa-9491-52f8-9c50-18fb61fb5a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5+2z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479490} {:answer/id #uuid "65bf5c02-8884-527a-8596-a864fde66c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+2z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479509} {:answer/id #uuid "cb387617-f782-5ecb-bb3d-85ab96c34d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5+2z}{z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479668} {:answer/id #uuid "efe243fe-0627-5bc7-ac0c-58cbf7e19251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5+5z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c4226aa-9491-52f8-9c50-18fb61fb5a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5+2z}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479490} :answer-field/id #uuid "112af529-727f-510a-876d-604ecec5f0bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61eea428-2437-5847-999f-591f4a5a456c" :question/math-academy-id "q-67242" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{z(5+2z)}{3z}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z(5+2z)}{3z}=\\frac{z\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $z$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{z}\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot \\cancel{z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5+2z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{5+2z}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38eb9547-3f69-5f6b-a8e3-7034b97a433a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "081c1c0a-d0e8-54bb-88e9-73fe216a5ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(x-1)}{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479371} {:answer/id #uuid "28b2884a-06e6-568f-b579-7f8512014993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479420} {:answer/id #uuid "e670a8e1-db7a-57a0-a060-c21171cecfbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(x-1)}{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479698} {:answer/id #uuid "ef34483e-106e-5818-a18b-e61e50d39479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(x-1)}{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479713} {:answer/id #uuid "f0cc50e3-0b5b-5c1a-862e-bef50e0df483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5(x-1)}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e670a8e1-db7a-57a0-a060-c21171cecfbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4(x-1)}{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479698} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2e828d3-cf66-52fd-bbb8-8a49e2cdb0b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85ebb17c-ed44-5cf2-a5c7-233470157f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-75369" :question/problem "$\\frac{12(x-1)}{15x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $12(x-1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1),$ and the $15x$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 5\\cdot x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12(x-1)}{15x}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1)}{3\\cdot 5\\cdot x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1)}{3\\cdot 5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot (x-1)}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (x-1)}{5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(x-1)}{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{4(x-1)}{5x}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054768 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38eb9547-3f69-5f6b-a8e3-7034b97a433a" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c48f353-e4e8-5167-a7c6-69ea1c45a203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a}{a+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479522} {:answer/id #uuid "96e5afa7-7dcc-5372-8334-82d34be0002e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{a+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479586} {:answer/id #uuid "ae72a19a-bc7f-5755-8385-3842bcb247e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{a+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479621} {:answer/id #uuid "efe9d29e-816e-5929-beb1-1ede407394ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a}{a+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479716} {:answer/id #uuid "fb512587-b0fa-560f-b1c8-d8ffb99f24fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a}{3(a+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c48f353-e4e8-5167-a7c6-69ea1c45a203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2a}{a+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479522} :answer-field/id #uuid "82d46f26-d6b2-510f-a75b-7142f40cc7f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cbe3ef2-930d-574e-83d8-95fc23d584d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-75370" :question/problem "$\\frac{18a}{9(a+2)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $18a$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a,$ and the $9(a+2)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18a}{9(a+2)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $3$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot a}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot (a+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot a}{a+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a}{a+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{2a}{a+2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19e1ac67-8538-5f72-bb0d-b7e4adda36a7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions with Common Binomial Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e2c46ec-a87f-561e-bb5a-e80045e5dcac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479544} {:answer/id #uuid "895c3ad6-e436-5b2f-a81b-70ac393a14ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479561} {:answer/id #uuid "92ca82ab-70b0-5f55-b824-d4d251229f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479580} {:answer/id #uuid "eddd6e42-563b-54b5-817b-fd7723075770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479710} {:answer/id #uuid "efdf0132-ec49-5bac-b7db-3e2bb81f938a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efdf0132-ec49-5bac-b7db-3e2bb81f938a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479714} :answer-field/id #uuid "b34e94ad-ff95-5978-bc6c-c7f7116d7c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b735757b-2dac-511b-87bc-3911444d7136" :question/math-academy-id "q-75375" :question/problem "$\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $3a(a-b)$ in the numerator becomes $3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b),$ and the $9(a-b)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)}=\\frac{3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b)}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(a-b)$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)} & =\\frac{3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b)}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}\\cdot a\\cdot \\cancel{(a-b)}}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{(a-b)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{a}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{a}{3}.$"}]}
